    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and all 3 tests pass (`pytest -q`, including a new one).\n\n**How it works:** it opens `mcp.Client(server, mode=\"legacy\", cache=None)`, which runs the server in the same process over in-memory streams, with no subprocess or sockets. Then it goes through the cases in order and calls `client.session.call_tool(...)`. It passes each result through the same `compare()` that `check_command` uses and collects the mismatches in the same order. No dependencies were added.\n\n**Why these settings:**\n- **`mode=\"legacy\"`:** by default, `Client` connects to an in-process server through a shortcut that skips the normal message exchange. Legacy mode sends real messages over in-memory streams and does the same `initialize` handshake as the stdio path. That way server authors get the same behaviour in pytest as the CLI gives over stdio.\n- **`client.session.call_tool` rather than `client.call_tool`:** this calls the same `ClientSession` method `check_command` uses. `Client.call_tool` adds some extra retry handling on top.\n- **`mcp.Client` rather than the in-memory transport directly:** the SDK's `InMemoryTransport` is in a private module (`mcp.client._memory`). Running the server on memory streams myself would also have meant reaching into a private attribute of `MCPServer`. `Client` is the public way to do this, and it accepts both `MCPServer` and low-level `Server` objects.\n\n**Test:** `tests/test_inproc.py` builds an `MCPServer` with `add` and `greet` tools. It checks that passing cases produce no mismatch, and that failing cases come back as mismatches in case order with the right `got` text."
  },
